<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish Empire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish Empire was one of the largest empires in history, built from the late fifteenth century with the conquest of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> financed its wars in Europe. It declined from the seventeenth century, losing most of its American colonies in the early nineteenth century and its last major overseas territories in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-empire.jpg" alt="Spanish Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The marriage of Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> created a personal union of their crowns that most scholars view as the foundation of the Spanish monarchy. Each kingdom kept its own administration, and the two ruled jointly as the Catholic Monarchs, a title given them by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was completed in 1492. Earlier expansion had begun with the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, finished by Castilian armies between 1478 and 1496; by 1504 more than 90 percent of the native Canarians had been killed or enslaved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in 1492, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> was founded in 1496.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their grandson Charles inherited the Castilian possessions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the Aragonese lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, beginning the rule of the Spanish Habsburgs. His son Philip II oversaw the colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1565 and annexed Portugal and its overseas empire in 1580. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> financed military campaigns, and Genoese bankers lent to the crown. Most conquests in the Americas were carried out by small groups of adventurers working under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system.</p><p>By the mid-17th century the empire strained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s economic, administrative and military resources. After the childless Charles II died in 1700, the Treaties of Utrecht of 1713 made Philip V of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> king and ceded Spain's possessions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to others and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Bourbons centralised power and reformed colonial rule. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> took the Spanish crown under his control, and the resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> led to about twenty new independent states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. By the mid-1820s Spain had lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and Guam in 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s trade system aimed to keep wealth within the empire, but smuggling was widespread, and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> paid for foreign soldiers and for goods made in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The empire left a lasting linguistic, religious, political and architectural legacy in the Western Hemisphere. Spanish spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and became the language of the successor republics, while many indigenous languages were lost as populations were decimated by war and disease.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its viceroys, councils and consulates provided models for the later <b>Spanish Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>New Spain was the most valuable part of the <b>Spanish Empire</b> and a hinge of the first global economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Spanish–American War, Spain ceded Cuba, Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines to the United States by the Treaty of Paris of 1898 for US$20 million, and Cuba, which had declared independence from the <b>Spanish Empire</b> that year, passed under an American military government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian Fernand Braudel spoke of an age of the Genoese from 1557 to 1627, when Genoese bankers handled the silver of the <b>Spanish Empire</b> and the exchange fairs at Piacenza became the clearing house of European finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Wilhelmus&quot; was written at the start of the Eighty Years' War, the Dutch Revolt against the <b>Spanish Empire</b>, at some time between April 1568 and the capture of Brielle on 1 April 1572, an event it does not mention.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish Dominican friar Domingo de Salazar was appointed the first Bishop of Manila, then newly annexed to the <b>Spanish Empire</b>; he served until 1594.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-6">6 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
